 threat  to our rights and it's time to overhaul  the business model that these companies  rely on how do you think we've gotten to  that point where what you say is  companies doing something we'd never  tolerate from a government how have we  gotten there have we learned that to  happen well that's a great question I  mean I think if you look at the  company's business model when they first  started they were much more privacy  respecting but as they have grown in  size and scale to the point where really  it is impossible to navigate the digital  world without coming under their  corporate surveillance machine and the  companies have been able to gradually  erode our privacy and threaten our  rights and all whole host of ways  without being held fully accountable and  I think you know we're increasingly now  seeing the the symptoms of this deeper  problem pretty much every week you know  just last week there was a Financial  Times investigation about how the  company's dozens of ad tech companies  including of course Google and Facebook  who sit at the heart of this system  we're receiving highly sensitive health  data from health websites including  medical symptoms diagnosis fertility  information that there's that is the  opposite of privacy and is an intrusion  into our private lives now want to take  a look at what else Facebook had to say  to say in response to your report here's  one bit of their response they say our  business model is what allows us to  offer an important service where people  can exercise foundational human rights  to have a voice freedom of expression  and be able to connect freedom of  association and assembly I mean I'm sure  you've also been following the situation  in Iran the internet there was  completely shut down during  anti-government protests many people  there could not use a tool like Facebook  or a tool like whatsapp to communicate  so on the other hand there are some  important pro-democracy features to  these  as well right yes absolutely and we  recognize that in fact that's exactly  the problem the scale of these platforms  they have become the the global public  square that billions of people rely on  to express themselves online to engage  with modern society that's the problem  in order to realize our human rights in  the digital age  we are forced to consent to a system  which is predicated on human rights  abuse and that is an impossible choice  for people to face and that's exactly  why you know governments need to address  the scale and dominance of these  platforms and you know radically  overhaul the business model so that we  can move towards a rights respecting  internet which really you know restores  the promise of the Internet for our  rights and for society of course some  governments have been taking action the  general data protection regulation gdpr  was enacted here in the EU last year  that could be one way to go about it Joe  Westby he's a researcher on technology  Big Data and human rights for Amnesty  International thank you very much thank  you so much
 

